::ENG3_U01_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary most precisely? {
=A. The speaker uses repetition to emphasize the audience's shared responsibility.#Correct. The sentence names the technique, effect, and audience clearly. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The speaker says the same thing and it is important.#This is understandable but too vague for academic explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The speaker has words that do a lot for people.#This does not identify the technique or effect. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The speaker makes everyone feel some way with stuff.#This wording is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] What does purpose mean in an academic response? {
~A. The exact words copied from a text#This describes quoted evidence, not purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The reason a writer or speaker communicates#Correct. Purpose is the reason for communication. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The group receiving the message#This defines audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The trustworthiness of a source#This describes credibility. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best completes the sentence? The example is _____ because it comes from a speaker who directly experienced the event. {
~A. audience#Audience refers to the people receiving a message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. credible#Correct. Evidence is credible when it is trustworthy and believable. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. vague#Vague means unclear or general, which does not fit the sentence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision best improves this vague sentence? Vague sentence: The writer uses a thing to make readers care. {
~A. The writer uses a thing to affect people.#This remains vague because thing and people are not precise. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The writer does writing that is emotional.#This is still general and does not name a technique. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The writer has an emotional thing in the text.#This wording is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The writer uses a personal example to appeal to the audience's emotions.#Correct. This revision identifies the technique and audience effect. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses the term evidence correctly? {
~A. The evidence is the people who hear the speech.#This defines audience, not evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The evidence is why the speaker is communicating.#This defines purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The evidence is the example the speaker uses to support the claim.#Correct. Evidence supports an idea or claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The evidence is the mood created by the ending.#Mood is an effect, not evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which phrase is the most precise replacement for good words in an analytical response? {
~A. nice talking#This phrase is informal and vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. strong stuff#This phrase is too general. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. helpful things#This does not identify a language choice. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. positive diction#Correct. Positive diction precisely names favorable word choice. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence would be strongest in a speaking note? {
=A. The speaker's purpose is to persuade the audience that change is possible.#Correct. The sentence is precise and clear enough to say aloud. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The person is trying to make people get it.#This is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The speaker does a thing about change.#This does not use academic vocabulary accurately. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The audience has some ideas from the speech.#This is unclear and does not explain purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best completes the sentence? The people a message is meant to reach are the _____. {
~A. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. audience#Correct. Audience means the people a writer or speaker is trying to reach. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. evidence#Evidence supports an idea. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. device#A device is a technique, not a group of people. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision uses academic vocabulary accurately? Vague sentence: The author makes the reader trust him. {
~A. The author makes trust happen in readers.#This remains vague and awkward. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The author is good and believable to people.#This is general and does not use the strongest academic term. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The author builds credibility with a specific personal example.#Correct. Credibility precisely names trustworthiness. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The author has an audience with examples.#This does not explain how trust is built. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which term means exact and specific? {
~A. Credible#Credible means trustworthy. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Precise#Correct. Precise means exact and specific. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Audience#Audience means the people receiving a message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Purpose#Purpose means the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence best explains a rhetorical device? {
~A. A rhetorical device is any sentence that is long.#Length alone does not make a rhetorical device. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. A rhetorical device is the audience of the speech.#Audience refers to people, not a technique. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. A rhetorical device is a technique used to affect an audience.#Correct. This definition matches the academic meaning. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. A rhetorical device is a source that proves a claim.#This describes evidence or source support, not a rhetorical device. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision best fits academic writing? Vague sentence: The quote shows the author wants something. {
~A. The quote shows stuff about what the author wants.#This is still vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The author wants a thing and the quote shows it.#This wording is informal and unclear. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The quote is good because it has purpose.#This does not explain the purpose precisely. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The evidence reveals the author's purpose by showing a call for action.#Correct. The revision uses evidence and purpose accurately. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses purpose correctly? {
=A. The author's purpose is to warn the audience about ignoring the problem.#Correct. The sentence identifies why the author communicates. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The author's purpose is the group reading the text.#This defines audience, not purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The author's purpose is the source used for evidence.#This confuses purpose with evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The author's purpose is a repeated phrase.#A repeated phrase may be a device, not the purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best completes the sentence? The speaker uses an anecdote as _____ to support the claim. {
~A. audience#Audience is the group receiving the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. evidence#Correct. An anecdote can function as evidence when it supports a claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. tone#Tone is attitude, not support for a claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence is too vague for English III analysis? {
~A. The author uses a credible example to support the claim.#This uses academic vocabulary accurately. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The speaker's purpose is to persuade the audience to act.#This clearly identifies purpose and audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The writer says some good things about the topic.#Correct. This sentence is too vague because it does not identify technique, purpose, or evidence. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The repeated phrase functions as a rhetorical device.#This uses academic vocabulary accurately. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision best improves this speaking note? Vague note: The text is trying to make people care. {
~A. The text is doing emotional stuff for people.#This remains vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The text says a lot so people care more.#This is still general and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The text has a purpose with readers.#This uses purpose unclearly. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The text appeals to the audience's emotions to support its purpose.#Correct. The revision uses precise vocabulary and remains speakable. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses claim accurately? {
=A. The claim is the writer's main position about community responsibility.#Correct. A claim is a position that can be supported. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The claim is the group listening to the speech.#This defines audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The claim is the trustworthiness of the speaker.#This describes credibility. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The claim is the technique used to create emphasis.#This describes a rhetorical device. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which option best replaces bad source in academic language? {
~A. not good thing#This wording is informal and vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. unreliable source#Correct. Unreliable source is precise academic language for a source that cannot be trusted. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. source with stuff#This is vague and does not explain the problem. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. audience source#This combines terms incorrectly. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence best connects vocabulary to audience? {
~A. The author uses evidence, and the evidence is words.#This does not explain audience effect. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The audience is a purpose that has evidence.#This confuses several terms. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The author uses direct address to make the audience feel personally involved.#Correct. The sentence names a technique and explains its audience effect. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The audience uses the author to make a device.#This is not a clear academic sentence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best completes the sentence? The speaker's repeated question is a rhetorical _____ that creates urgency. {
~A. audience#Audience is the group receiving the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. evidence#Evidence supports a claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. device#Correct. A repeated question can be a rhetorical device. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision is most precise? Vague sentence: The example makes the speech better. {
=A. The example strengthens the speaker's credibility by showing direct experience.#Correct. This explains how the example improves the speech. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The example is good and helps the speech.#This remains vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The example does something for the speech.#This does not explain the effect. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The example has a lot of helpful words.#This is unclear and imprecise. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses precise vocabulary for oral presentation? {
~A. I think the author does stuff with words.#This is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The author's diction helps the audience understand the serious tone.#Correct. This is precise and clear enough to say aloud. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The writer talks good about the idea.#This wording is informal and unclear. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The audience is where the evidence goes.#This misuses audience and evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence correctly explains credible evidence? {
~A. Credible evidence is evidence that sounds exciting.#Excitement does not make evidence trustworthy. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Credible evidence is evidence that is very long.#Length does not determine credibility. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Credible evidence is evidence that is trustworthy and relevant to the claim.#Correct. Credible evidence can be believed and supports the point. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Credible evidence is evidence that avoids all details.#Evidence usually needs details to support an idea. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which academic term best replaces people in this sentence? The speaker wants people to accept the claim. {
~A. evidence#Evidence supports a claim; it does not refer to people. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. audience#Correct. Audience is the academic term for the people the speaker wants to reach. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. device#Device is a technique used to affect an audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence best shows mastery of academic vocabulary? {
~A. The writer has good stuff and the audience gets it.#This is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The audience is evidence because the purpose is credible.#This misuses the vocabulary terms. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The writer uses credible evidence to persuade the audience that the claim is reasonable.#Correct. The sentence uses credible evidence, persuade, audience, and claim accurately. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The purpose is a device that proves the audience.#This confuses purpose, device, and audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
